in our series i on earth a new report  shows an alarming drop in worldwide  shark populations this unprecedented  global study finds  sharks have simply vanished from many  places  the research was backed by funding from  the late microsoft co-founder paul allen  before the pandemic in quarantine mark  phillips spent a day at sea with its  lead authors for a story you'll see only  on cbs this morning  good morning we're off the coast of  florida near palm beach  and we're hunting for sharks not because  they're in the news for all the wrong  reasons because in a lot of places  there are too few of them for our own  good  the authors of the study use a simple  contraption with the complicated name  made it remote underwater video or bruv  bruv for short a cage with bait in it at  one end  and a camera at the other okay going in  the water  damien chapman and mike height house  have been part of the most comprehensive  worldwide shark survey ever conducted  did you invent this kind of photo trap  or it was a  pretty pretty common usage say we  perfected it  yeah you drop it overboard and lower it  to the ocean floor  the project they call fin print has done  this over  15 thousand times over four years in 58  countries  the idea is to leave the bruv down there  for an hour and see how many sharks show  up  sometimes in some places quite a few  sometimes none of the almost 400 reefs  that have been surveyed around the world  20 percent of them we didn't see a  single shark but is that in itself an  indicator of the problem  absolutely caught by fishermen sometimes  accidentally  often on purpose to satisfy the growing  shark meat market  populations have crashed she'd come in  right here  in this drop off the florida coast the  pickings were slim  no shock nurse shirt yep big one  sometimes they'll just come in have a  sniff and then just head right away  in fact a loggerhead turtle seemed more  interested in this free lunch than the  sharks were  but by using the same setup recording  for the same amount of time on each drop  around the world  the researchers have been able to draw  the first comprehensive  database conclusions about worldwide  shark numbers  do you think the population is under  crisis oh i absolutely think this is a  crisis  but a different kind of crisis one with  the new term  the word extinction gets thrown around  verge of extinction virtual extinction  what have you do you use that phrase  well for our survey we use the term  functional extinction  functional extinction meaning there may  be some sharks around  but there are not enough of them to  perform their essential role  of controlling other marine populations  you might think hey let's get rid of  predators that eat fish so we can have  more fish  but it actually doesn't work that way  you lose predators their prey explode  and they eat themselves out of house and  home  when you lose top predators ecosystems  tend to start to unravel  there are bright spots in this study  where marine protection zones have been  introduced  or fishing restrictions tighten so that  fewer sharks are caught  their numbers have begun to come back  some people will be sitting at home  saying  why are we worried about these sharks  they don't get a great press well sharks  definitely need a new pr agency  but what we're really seeing is that you  need to have healthy shark populations  you need to have healthy reefs and  that's going to ultimately help people  too  this study isn't only about gathering  knowledge it's about gathering  ammunition a way to convince governments  and fish and communities around the  world  to do something sharks don't get great  press gail but we sure do need them  yeah i know and i didn't really realize  that until you start really reading and  learning about them he's absolutely  right  don't don't disparage the sharps they  are badly badly needed and make a big  difference in the environment  even though they are very scary
 

